<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burra, South Australia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burra is a town in the mid-north of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, founded after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> was discovered there in 1845. Its Monster Mine, worked largely by Cornish and Welsh miners, was for a time one of the richest metal mines in Australia and helped rescue the young colony's finances. Mining ended in 1877, and the town's stone cottages and mine buildings are now heritage sites.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burra-south-australia.jpg" alt="Burra, South Australia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: mikecogh · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burra_Town_Hall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Burra lies east of the Clare Valley in the Bald Hills range, part of the northern Mount Lofty Ranges, on Burra Creek and a few kilometres inside Goyder's Line, at the edge of land fit for farming. The ore body, up to 70 metres wide, lay between two faults in broken dolomite and consisted mainly of green malachite and blue azurite. The original inhabitants were the Ngadjuri people, whose first Western contact came in 1839; European diseases depleted them severely, and rock art and burial sites remain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 9 June 1845 the shepherd William Streair brought ore samples to Henry Ayers, secretary of the South Australian Mining Association. The 20,000-acre survey was split by lot: wealthy capitalists, the nobs, took the southern half and opened the unsuccessful Princess Royal Mine, while the shopkeepers and merchants of the association, the snobs, opened the Burra Burra Mine in the north. Mining began on 29 September 1845, and until 1860 it was the largest metals mine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, producing about 50,000 tonnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> by 1877 and paying under 100 shareholders £826,586 in dividends.</p><p>Kooringa, laid out by George Strickland Kingston in 1846, was the first company township in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>; the association granted no freehold there, so other townships grew up, among them government-owned Redruth and Llwchwr, built for a smelting works brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swansea">Swansea</a>. In 1851 more than a third of the people lived in dugouts in the creek banks. Ayers held wages down, and the miners' strike of 1848 was the first industrial strike in South Australia. Employment peaked at 1,208 in 1859, and after the closure the town became the colony's main sheep trading centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Burra is a centre for the surrounding farms and one of the best-preserved towns of the Victorian era in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, declared a State Heritage Town in 1994. Its townships were joined as one town in July 1940. Survivals include the water-filled open cut, mine chimneys, the Redruth Gaol and miners' dugouts and cottages. The Burra Charter on heritage management takes its name from a conference held here in 1979.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burra, South Australia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Early financial crisis forced Governor George Grey to impose austerity in the 1840s, but copper finds at Kapunda and <b>Burra</b> and wheat farming brought prosperity.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-south-australia">Province of South Australia</a></li></ul></section>
